window.COACH = {"page": "software", "title": "Digital Twin", "prizeName": "Best Software, Best Measurement", "summary": {"verdict": "On evidence this page is already past the winners. A null-model baseline for the trust layer, a leave-one-arm-out table, a forecast fixed by hash before the batch and a sensor calibration that failed and is reported anyway are moves that none of the eight Best Software winners and none of the six Best Measurement winners we read attempted. Two things hold it back. The software ballot's other four questions go unanswered because the page has no install route, no component list, no log format and no protocol on it, and the licence label contradicts the repository's own LICENSE file. And the page disagrees with other ReLEAF pages about its own facts: ten of the notes below and about ten more items in extras quote another one of our pages contradicting this one, including the strain of the 434-hour run and whether the 24-hour row behind R5 is a result at all.", "strengths": ["Provenance on every value. Tagging each channel in-line, commanded, soft, bench tested, planned or external, and applying it consistently across both tables, is a data standard, and nothing in the winners' pages matches it.", "Validation against something. R3 scores the trust layer against a naive step detector on the same data, R4 scores the Kalman filter against persistence at four horizons, and R5 holds out each light arm in turn. No winning software page compares its tool to a null model.", "A prediction registered before the experiment. prereg_forecast_20261002.json with its SHA-256, acceptance criteria in a table, and the promise that the score goes in whichever way it falls.", "R6 is the best-reported section on the page. It gives its n, its sampling rate and its three sites before any result, states the bias against the reference it was measured against, and reports the wetness channel as untested instead of as dry.", "Limits with mechanisms. Eight bullets, each carrying a figure or a named cause, and the rule that nothing counts as validated unless it was scored on data it was not fitted to, including where the page itself fails that rule (\"No point was held out.\")."], "priorities": ["Reconcile the page against the rest of the wiki before the freeze. Ten notes here and about ten more items in extras quote another ReLEAF page contradicting this one. Three are serious: Hardware and Engineering call the 434-hour run WB800 where you call it 168; Measurement's table for the 19 September plate run gives the green arms at 0.008 to 0.083 at 24 h and calls that row a handling fault, which is the row R5 rests on; and Measurement's LPA procedure says the plate was incubated at 37 degrees, which removes the registered forecast's reason for not applying its own criterion.", "Put a protocol on the page for at least the TiO2 calibration, and a floor under the photometer. The silica paragraph shows you can write one: step size, range, volume, matrix, instrument, logging interval. The TiO2 run is the page's headline calibration and it is one sentence. Measurement ballot questions 1 and 2 ask for nothing else, and the OD 1.2 ceiling is defended while the blank level is never stated, on a page where two runs start near zero.", "Answer the four software ballot questions the page currently skips. A clone URL and a command in the body, a three-sentence component list saying what runs on the board, in the backend and in the browser, the log's column list with its units, and two lines of the serial protocol. All four describe work that is already done, and the repository is also the eligibility item.", "Fix the licence. Two figure labels say MIT; the repository LICENSE puts the source code under CC BY 4.0, which is not an OSI-approved licence and so does not satisfy Best Software eligibility. Give the licence its own heading and put a matching LICENSE file beside the code.", "Move n to where each result is stated. \"n = 1 almost everywhere\" in the limits is honest and it is in the wrong place. R1 is eight settings measured once, R2 is one afternoon, R3 is one run with one event of each kind, R5 is one plate. Only R6 and the dilution check get this right today."], "ballot": [{"question": "How well is the software compatible with, and does it leverage, existing synthetic biology standards? (e.g. SBOL, other RFCs, data formats, etc.)", "status": "missing", "where": "", "note": "The provenance scheme is a data standard and the page never names a format, a column, a unit or a header line; gap_s appears once in a subordinate clause."}, {"question": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?", "status": "strong", "where": "3 Results (R3, R4, R5) and 4 Registered forecast", "note": "A null-model baseline, a leave-one-arm-out table and a hash-registered forecast put this question ahead of all eight winners."}, {"question": "Can the software be useful to other projects?", "status": "partial", "where": "7 For other teams", "note": "Five transferable methods, one with the 434-hour batch as a test case, against reuse language the casebook calls the weak kind and no user outside the team."}, {"question": "How well can the software be integrated with external tools/software applications? (APIs, packages, etc.)", "status": "missing", "where": "7 For other teams", "note": "The two-line ASCII protocol is named once in a bullet and never shown; no endpoint, no log line, no API, no harness command."}, {"question": "Is the software user-friendly?", "status": "partial", "where": "5 The software that runs the reactor", "note": "Two live embeds and an explanation behind every information mark, against no walkthrough of either interface and no route for a reader to run anything."}, {"question": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)", "status": "missing", "where": "5 The software that runs the reactor", "note": "No clone URL, no component list, no requirements line, no test description, and a licence that appears only as a figure label and contradicts the repository LICENSE."}, {"question": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?", "status": "partial", "where": "3 Results (R2 silica repeat, R6)", "note": "The silica paragraph and R6 can be repeated from the page. R1, the TiO2 run and R3 cannot: no replicate count, no volumes, no waits."}, {"question": "Are the methods or protocols described well?", "status": "partial", "where": "R2, The silica repeat, 10 October", "note": "One real protocol on the page. The headline TiO2 calibration is \"cumulative TiO2 additions gave 45 paired points\", with no dose, volume, blank or settling time."}, {"question": "Could it be useful to other projects?", "status": "strong", "where": "7 For other teams", "note": "Give every sensor a companion that cannot see what it measures, demonstrated twice on this page, with a dataset attached. This is the page's most reusable idea."}, {"question": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?", "status": "partial", "where": "R2, R3, R6", "note": "Calibration against a known dose and a failed sensor calibration reported in the section that uses the instrument, against no limit of detection, no residual plot, and a direct conflict with the Measurement page over whether the photometer is calibrated at all."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1 Introduction", "2 What the reactor measures now", "3 Results (R1 to R6)", "4 Registered forecast", "5 The software that runs the reactor", "6 Gaps and limits", "7 For other teams"], "winners": ["What it is and who needs it", "Why it exists, with a number", "Architecture, components with their jobs", "Method or model per component", "Install: requirements, then commands, then the URL you end up with", "Usage: a numbered walkthrough or a worked example", "Validation", "Limits and future work", "Repository, licence, references"], "gap": "Sections 3 and 4 are a deeper validation chapter than any winner wrote, and they sit where five of the eight winners put install and usage. The page has no architecture section, no install section and no usage section at all, so a judge cannot tell what runs where or get the code, and section 5 carries all three jobs in prose. Winners spend a third to a half of their words on architecture, install and usage; at about 6,000 words we are longer than six of the eight and almost all of it is results."}, "extras": ["Run 4 starts at two values. The run table says \"then 102 h from 0.84 to 3.77\"; R4 says \"OD600 rose from 0.83 to 3.77\" for the same file. Fix both to the logged value.", "R2's new reconcile fold is progress and does not finish the job. \"Over the 16 points above 30 mg\" now scopes the R² 0.85 correctly, and the fold says Measurement \"quotes the whole series\". But Measurement, Hardware and Engineering all still claim the agreement holds \"across the full range\" at \"R² > 0.999\", and our Results page sides with this page (\"the BioDrop returns R2 = 0.8501\"). Four pages, two stories. Settle it and delete both reconcile folds.", "The light array has two names. The channel table says \"Characterised on the DiOPAL plate apparatus\"; R5, Measurement and the Hardware page for the same rig all call it the light-programmable array or LPA, and that Hardware page never uses the word DiOPAL. Use one name.", "A past date written as pending. Run 5's row and section 4 both say \"Due to end about 19:00 on 9 October\". Replace with \"Ran 8 to 9 October; the score goes into section 4 when the analysis is finished.\"", "Burst size disagrees with Hardware. This page says \"sixteen more\" readings per burst; Hardware photometer says \"A burst of fourteen. We take 14 lux readings in a short burst\", and Engineering's \"42.9 % and 14.3 % are exactly six and two readings out of fourteen\" only works at fourteen. Measurement agrees with sixteen. One of the three is wrong.", "Culture volume disagrees with three pages. Section 5 says \"with 1 L of culture at 22 °C\"; Hardware bioreactor says \"300 mL working volume\", Biomanufacturing \"about 400 mL working volume\", Engineering \"the reactor's 300 mL\". This page is the only one at 1 L.", "Loop temperature is tagged in-line while the same row says \"or a value typed by the operator\". Your own list defines an operator-set value as commanded, and section 6 says the 8 October batch carried a typed 37 °C. Tag it \"in-line or commanded, per row\".", "The operating point moved and the page does not say so. R1 says \"263 mL/min has been the operating point since\"; section 5 reports the 8 October batch at \"loop flow 386.9 mL/min at PWM 145\".", "Design flux is never given. \"36 times the design flux\" implies a design flux of 2 LMH; Biomanufacturing works from \"an operating flux of 5\" LMH, which would make the typed value about fourteen times. State the design flux.", "The 22 °C plateau disagrees by a transposition. Limits say \"plateaus at OD 1.454\"; Biomanufacturing uses \"OD600 1.54\" as the carrying capacity in both its oxygen and its perfusion arithmetic. Whichever is right, the other page propagates the wrong one.", "The live weather source disagrees with Math Model. This page says \"Open-Meteo, hourly, five days back and three ahead\"; Math Model says \"Central Weather Administration (中央氣象署) station data for live use\".", "Chars is credited by first name only. \"our instructor Chars wrote\" should be \"our instructor Chars Hsieh wrote\" on first mention. Our roster lists his role as Wet Lab Instructor, so if he wrote the rig application, say that here, because no other page credits anyone with it.", "kLa 45.7 and 60 h⁻¹ appear nowhere else on the wiki. Biomanufacturing derives a required kLa of 13.2 h⁻¹. Say what the two numbers are estimates of, or a reader will try to reconcile them with 13.2.", "The TiO2 run is described differently on three pages. Here it is one 23 August series of \"45 paired points\"; Hardware describes two TiO2 runs of about forty and twenty points with two excluded readings; Results gives the reservoir as 400 mL where Hardware, Measurement and Engineering say 500 mL. No page states 45.", "Source filenames use three date formats in one caption: \"260610 test1 no stir.xlsx\", \"0721, test3 20 day.csv\", \"20260902\". Keep the filenames, which no winner does, and normalise the format.", "\"It updates with each reading without refitting\" opens a paragraph whose nearest noun is inside a closed fold. Name the Kalman filter.", "\"the eight robust estimators\" is the only mention of them on the page and assumes the reader knows what they are. Give them a clause.", "\"fourteen-fold\" and \"a factor of 11\" describe different things four paragraphs apart in the June fold: the flask's own growth, and the gap between the two vessels at 24 h. Say which is which.", "Figure 10's caption says \"the log carries no clock\" while R6's opening says the runs were \"logged every five seconds\". Both are true. Write \"carries a sample interval and no timestamp\".", "The chromoprotein idea in section 5 raises a question and leaves it open. Move it to Gaps and limits as a row, or state what a second peak would have to satisfy.", "The Beer-Lambert limit bullet carries two optical configurations, two ceilings and a third point about induction light. Split it into three bullets."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics", "hardware/diopal", "hardware/hydroponics"], "wiki": "655c7af, 2026-10-12", "root": "../", "notes": [{"id": "dt-a01", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "No result rests on one instrument", "title": "This claim is wider than the page", "body": "Your own last sentence says \"almost every result rests on one run\", and R5 rests on one plate reader while R4's forecast rests on the photometer alone. Three of the seven rows below have a single instrument behind them. Narrow the claim to the results that really carry a cross-check.", "after": "Four results carry a second, independent check:", "excerpts": [], "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a02", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "a reference beam that never sees the culture separates lamp drift from growth", "title": "Four cross-checks in one sentence", "body": "This sentence runs for about seventy words and lists four different cross-checks before the reader has met any of them. It is the second thing a judge reads. Give the principle its own short sentence and let the four checks follow as a list.", "after": "No result rests on one instrument. A reference beam that never sees the culture separates lamp drift from growth. Wall shear is computed twice, from pressure and from flow. The photometer is scored against a known TiO₂ dose. The field leaf sensor is scored against an air probe in the same air.", "excerpts": [], "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a03", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the photometer was calibrated against a known TiO₂ dose", "title": "Our two pages disagree about calibration", "body": "This page says the photometer \"was calibrated against a known TiO₂ dose\". Our Measurement page says the opposite about the same instrument: \"Our central contribution is a validated, calibration-free in-line OD600 measurement device\" and \"Therefore, we treat calibration as unnecessary.\" A measurement judge who opens both pages sees one experiment reported two ways. Agree on one account with the Measurement team and write it in the same words on both pages.", "after": "the photometer was scored against a known TiO₂ dose", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a04", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "That layer gave 26.7 hours of warning before the OD channel failed", "title": "Which layer is that layer", "body": "The sentence before this one names four separate cross-checks, so \"that layer\" has nothing single to point back to. Name the trust layer, and say the warning came from one event on one run, which is what your limits already admit.", "after": "The trust layer gave 26.7 hours of warning before the OD channel failed, on one event in one 434-hour run.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL attaches n to the headline number itself, so the reader never has to look for it.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a05", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Both interfaces run live on this page", "title": "Say there is software to download", "body": "A Best Software judge reads this sheet first and leaves it without learning that there is code, a licence or a repository. You have both: the rig app ships as one file served by the board, and the rewrite is MIT. Add one clause naming the licence and the repository the code sits in.", "after": "Both interfaces run live on this page, and both are MIT licensed at [repository URL].", "excerpts": ["software-2024-og-unm-01"], "excerpt_note": "UniMuenster's abstract names the platforms and how the tool is distributed, so a judge who will never install it still knows what it is.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a06", "type": "praise", "anchor": "26.7 h of warning before the OD channel failed", "title": "The status word in every result row", "body": "Your Key results ledger gives every row a one-word status: Worked, Partial, Calibrated, Bench, Pending. A judge can read the whole claim list in ten seconds and knows which rows to trust. Keep this column exactly as it is, and keep Partial and Bench honest when the numbers improve.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-03"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius counts four of five planned features shipped and names the one that did not. Your status column does the same job in one word per row.", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a07", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Slope 1.018 against BioDrop, R² 0.995, on a known dose", "title": "Two different scorings in one row", "body": "The slope of 1.018 and R² 0.995 come from regressing the photometer on the BioDrop up to OD 1.2. The known-dose scoring is a separate analysis with different numbers. This row reads as though one fit did both. Pick the number you want in the headline and say what it was measured against.", "after": "Slope 1.018 against the BioDrop to OD 1.2, R² 0.995; both instruments also scored against a known dose", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-001", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Nobody will be there to take samples or read every value, so the digital twin makes the decisions", "title": "The page later says a person decides", "body": "Your Current limits say the opposite: \"every decision about the culture, above all induction, still goes through a person\", and the abstract says \"The loop is not closed.\" A judge will read this opening sentence as a claim the rest of the page withdraws. Put it in the tense you mean.", "after": "Nobody will be there to take samples or read every value, so the twin has to turn the instrument readings into a recommendation a farmer can act on.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-002", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Without manual sampling, the reactor's own instruments are the only record of the batch, and they drift as they wear", "title": "The whole page argued in one line", "body": "This is the best sentence on the page. It gives the reader the problem (no sampling), the consequence (the instruments are the only record) and the catch (they drift) in one clause each, and everything after it is evidence for this line. Do not touch it.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-01", "measurement-2023-ug-wm-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners open on the difficulty instead of the tool. Vilnius: someone had to sit with the bioreactor. William-and-Mary: soil interferes with every fluorescence method. Your sentence does the same work in half the words."}, {"id": "dt-003", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The twin also decides when to switch on green light to start protectant production, which protectant, and how much", "title": "One paragraph doing five jobs", "body": "This paragraph covers regional data, field sensors, local data, the induction rule, the forecast, the prior and a future assay. The reader cannot hold it. Split it in two: what the twin reads, then what it decides from it.", "after": "The twin reads three kinds of data. Regional data (the weather forecast, crop-stress risk and the Geospatial Analysis maps) show when and where crops will come under stress. Field sensors beside the crop measure leaf temperature, leaf wetness, and air temperature and humidity (R6). Local data from BR-01 show how much biomass is in the loop and whether those readings can be trusted (R3).\n\nFrom these it decides when to switch on green light, which protectant and how much. Induction makes sense when the field is heading into stress and the culture can respond, and the forecast (R4) and growth prior (R5) say whether the batch will be ready in time.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-004", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Every value is tagged by how it was obtained:", "title": "Call the provenance scheme a standard", "body": "This list is a data standard and the page never says so. One sentence here, naming which formats the logs are written in and which published standards they do and do not follow, answers the software ballot's first question, which is currently your weakest. You already know the answer: CSV with a fixed header, no SBOL, no RFC.", "after": "Every value is tagged by how it was obtained. Every logged row carries its tag, so a reader of the CSV can separate measured values from commanded and soft ones. The logs follow no published synthetic biology standard; they are plain CSV with a fixed header, which any spreadsheet or pandas script opens.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mun-01", "software-2023-ug-fud-01"], "excerpt_note": "Neither winner claims to be standards-compliant. They name the formats and the tools that open them. That is what question one rewards.", "ballot": "How well is the software compatible with, and does it leverage, existing synthetic biology standards? (e.g. SBOL, other RFCs, data formats, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-005", "type": "praise", "anchor": "in-line A sensor on the loop of culture pumped around the bioreactor", "title": "A provenance word for every value", "body": "Tagging every channel in-line, commanded, soft, bench tested, planned or external is the strongest thing on this page for both ballots. It lets a judge see at a glance which numbers are measured and which are hoped for, and you apply it consistently in both tables. No winner we read has anything like it.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mun-01"], "excerpt_note": "Munich answers the standards question by naming formats a reader can check. Your provenance tags are the same kind of answer, and they are yours."}, {"id": "dt-006", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Photometer: amber LED, 2 mm flow cuvette, sample and reference lux sensors", "title": "Say the wavelength, not the colour", "body": "\"Amber\" is a colour; OD600 is a wavelength claim. Our Hardware photometer page gives the number and ties the two together: \"600 nm sits in a strong amber, right in the middle of the green (~535 nm) and red (~672 nm) peaks of CcaS\". Put the number here, because a judge checking whether your OD600 is really an OD600 looks at this row.", "after": "Photometer: 600 nm amber LED, 2 mm flow cuvette, sample and reference lux sensors", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-007", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Lumen ΔP, TMP, fouling ratio", "title": "TMP is never written out", "body": "The row above this one spells out \"trans-membrane pressure\", and this row abbreviates it without ever joining the two. TMP then appears six more times on the page. Our judges read many pages a day and are not bioprocess engineers.", "after": "Lumen ΔP, trans-membrane pressure (TMP), fouling ratio", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-008", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Today's rig software reads none of these.", "title": "A fact that lands before its subject", "body": "\"These\" has no referent yet; the table comes after this sentence. Let the reader see the channels first, then tell them nothing reads them.", "after": "These are the channels outside the reactor: what the plants are experiencing, and what the weather is about to do. Today's rig software reads none of them. They belong to the next interface, and the leaf and air sensors have been on a bench but never on a plant (R6).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-009", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The interface applies the BLASTAM rule for leaf blast [7]: a wet period counts when the leaf stays wet 10 h at 22–25 °C", "title": "Six thresholds for a rule that fires nothing", "body": "This fold spends a hundred words and three references on the exact hour-and-temperature table for a rule that your last sentence says \"triggers nothing\". Keep why the rule is there and the fact that it is unscored, and move the threshold table to wherever the rule is implemented.", "after": "The interface applies the BLASTAM rule for leaf blast [7], which counts a wet period from leaf wetness hours and temperature, using the sensor for past hours and forecast humidity for future ones [8, 9]. The sensor is still on a bench, the rule is unscored on Taiwanese fields, and ACC deaminase is untested against blast, so blast risk is shown to the grower and triggers nothing.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-010", "type": "cut", "anchor": "BR-01 has grown B. subtilis five times. Runs 1 and 2 were June prototypes sampled by hand against a shake flask", "title": "The abstract already said this", "body": "\"BR-01 has grown B. subtilis five times\" opens the abstract word for word. The chapter eyebrow above already says \"Five runs\". Start on the thing the abstract did not say.", "after": "Runs 1 and 2 were June prototypes sampled by hand against a shake flask; from July the in-line photometer took over. Figure 1 shows the four finished runs.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-011", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Stopped by a pump leak. 2 mL of shell-side liquid grew no colonies.", "title": "Every run ends in what it changed", "body": "The \"What it gave\" column is a troubleshooting log in disguise: symptom, what you found, what you changed next. This is the most reusable thing in the whole results section, because the next team reads it to avoid your week. Keep the \"Next:\" clauses.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-og-aalto-04"], "excerpt_note": "Aalto logs each failure as symptom, change, result, and the casebook calls it the most reusable passage on their page. Your run table already does this.", "ballot": "Could it be useful to other projects?"}, {"id": "dt-012", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "16 August 2026: water at 22 °C, eight pump settings, once with the module fitted and once with a jumper in its place", "title": "Say how many times each setting was read", "body": "You give the date, the fluid, the temperature and the eight settings, which is most of a protocol. The one thing a team repeating this needs and cannot get is how many readings you took at each setting and how long you waited for the pressure to settle. Both are in your log.", "after": "16 August 2026: water at 22 °C, eight pump settings, each held [number] s and read [number] times, once with the module fitted and once with a jumper in its place.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "KULeuven lists every standard concentration and the matrix they were made in, so the curve can be remade. One line of that kind here fixes R1 for both measurement questions.", "ballot": "Are the methods or protocols described well?"}, {"id": "dt-013", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The 25 August progress report gives 0.084 bar and 0.90–0.96× theory for the same measurement", "title": "Two unresolved notes left in public", "body": "The page now carries two folds called \"To reconcile before the freeze\", this one and the one in R2. Each tells a judge that we have two answers for one measurement and have not chosen. In both cases you have chosen: here the next sentence says the figures are the later analysis, and in R2 the scoped figure is the right one. Put the decision in the body as a reason, and keep a fold only for what is still genuinely open.", "after": "Both figures come from one spreadsheet; the 0.096 bar and 1.03× quoted here are from the later re-analysis, which corrected [what changed]. The 25 August progress report's 0.084 bar and 0.90–0.96× are superseded.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-014", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Wall shear can be computed from the pressure drop or from the flow rate, and the two agree to 2.8%", "title": "Our notebook says ten per cent", "body": "Our Dry Lab Notebook reports the same cross-check from the same measurement as agreeing \"to within 10 per cent\". The whole argument in section 5 is that October's 20% disagreement is a fault because August agreed to 2.8%. At 10% it is much less of an outlier. Settle which number is right before the freeze, because this one sentence carries the clogging indicator.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-015", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "On 23 August 2026, cumulative TiO₂ additions gave 45 paired points between the in-line photometer and the BioDrop spectrophotometer", "title": "This calibration has no protocol", "body": "This is the page's headline calibration and the whole method is \"cumulative TiO₂ additions\". A team repeating it cannot get the dose per addition, the loop volume, the blank, or the wait between additions. Both measurement ballot questions turn on exactly this, and the silica paragraph below shows you can write it. Write this one the same way.", "after": "On 23 August 2026 the loop was dosed with TiO₂ in [mass] steps from [mass] to [mass] in [volume] of [matrix], blanked on [blank], read on the BioDrop and in line after [wait] at each step, giving 45 paired points.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "KULeuven's curve can be remade from one sentence: eight concentrations, the stock, the matrix and why that matrix. That is the shape your TiO2 sentence is missing.", "ballot": "Are the methods or protocols described well?"}, {"id": "dt-016", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Scoring both against the known dose, not against each other, showed that both degrade above 30 mg in different ways", "title": "Calibrating against truth, not an instrument", "body": "Scoring both instruments against a known dose, and finding the cheap one more linear than the fifteen-thousand-dollar one, is a better calibration design than anything in the six winning measurement pages. The clause \"not against each other\" is what makes it land. Keep it.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04", "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "Austin calibrates to purchased standards and never scores the reference itself. You score both. That is the step past the winner.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-017", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Up to OD 1.2 the photometer regresses on the BioDrop with slope 1.018, R² 0.995, residual SD 0.024 OD", "title": "The ceiling is defended, the floor is not", "body": "You defend the top of the range carefully and never state the bottom. Run 4 starts \"from near zero\" and the 8 October batch opens at OD600 0.1758, both close to the floor. You have the blank readings and the residual SD, so you can say where a reading stops meaning anything.", "after": "Up to OD 1.2 the photometer regresses on the BioDrop with slope 1.018, R² 0.995, residual SD 0.024 OD. Below OD [number] the reading is inside the blank scatter of [number] and is reported as a floor, not a value.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02", "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02"], "excerpt_note": "Austin states OD600 0.1 as the point its plate reader stops being trustworthy. Aalto reads its curve out loud at both ends. One line each.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-018", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "TiO₂ shows linearity, not cell number.", "title": "True, and the reader is left there", "body": "This is the right caveat and it stops too early. The reader wants to know what would turn one into the other, and your Must-have table already answers it. Point at the answer.", "after": "TiO₂ shows linearity, not cell number. Converting the in-line reading to biomass needs the plate-to-reactor dilution series listed in Must have, yet to have, so every OD on this page is an optical reading and not a cell count.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-019", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "On 10 October 2026 the same loop ran the calibration iGEM's protocol actually asks for", "title": "Three pages say this run never happened", "body": "Our Results page says \"The run has not happened. There is no SiO2 data in the archive: no regression, no R2, no graph\", Measurement calls it \"a written protocol with no results behind it yet\", and Engineering lists it as a cycle that has not been run. Our Hardware photometer page carries the same numbers you do, so you and Hardware are current and three pages are stale. Tell those three teams, or a judge opening Results will read your best protocol as invented.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-020", "type": "praise", "anchor": "silica in 2.5 mg steps from 2.5 to 100 mg in 500 mL of 1% SDS, read at every step on the BioDrop", "title": "This one is a real protocol", "body": "Step size, range, volume, matrix, instrument, logging interval and duration, all in one sentence. A judge can repeat this. It is the only measurement on the page that can be run from the page, so it shows you already know how to write the others.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "This sentence is doing the same work as KULeuven's calibration passage, which is the most copyable thing on any of the six winning pages.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-021", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "It rose from 289 to 324 lux over the first two hours and then held flat, a 12% change with no fault behind it", "title": "The reference channel in two scales", "body": "R3's table reports the same channel stepping from 26.67 to 25.83 lux. Here it sits near 300. The difference is the optical configuration, and nothing says so where the numbers appear, so a reader thinks one of them is wrong.", "after": "It rose from 289 to 324 lux over the first two hours and then held flat, a 12% change with no fault behind it. These lux values are on the splitter build; R3's run 3 figures of 26.67 lux are the earlier single-path build, so the two are not comparable.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-022", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The run carries its own check. At 137.6 min the suspension was diluted and then restored", "title": "A check that needs no known dose", "body": "Diluting and restoring to test proportionality is clever, and the numbers are reported the way a judge wants them: three levels, each with its SD and its number of readings. This is the only place on the page where n sits next to the result. Keep it, and copy the habit into R1, R2 and R5.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL carries n, the correction and every p value in the sentence that makes the claim. Your dilution check already reads like that.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-023", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "B. subtilis 168 growth log with the photometer", "title": "The heading hides the result", "body": "Your contents rail calls this R3 Trust layer, and the section's finding is that the trust layer caught a failing instrument. This heading names the dataset instead. Give the heading the finding.", "after": "The trust layer on a 434-hour growth log", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-024", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "21 July to 8 August 2026: 434 hours of B. subtilis 168 at 22 °C, 2,671 valid rows, no sample drawn", "title": "Our Hardware pages name a different strain", "body": "You say 168. Our Hardware bioreactor page says of the same run: \"A continuous run of engineered B. subtilis WB800 at 22 °C, drawn from the bioreactor loop\", and Hardware photometer and Engineering say WB800 too. Results and Biomanufacturing back you (\"Every reactor number on this wiki is strain 168, run 4 included\"). Five pages, two strains, one run. Fix this first: it decides whether the page's main dataset is a wild-type or an engineered culture.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-025", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A check is an alarm when its score exceeds the 99.5th percentile of the healthy first 120 hours", "title": "The threshold is fixed before the test", "body": "You set the alarm threshold on the healthy first 120 hours and then score the remaining 314 against it. That is the difference between a detector and a line drawn after the fact, and a judge who knows the difference will look for this sentence. It is also what makes R3 reusable.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-04"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg states its two pass criteria before giving the score. Fixing the rule first is the same move, and you do it on a window of the data instead of on the whole run.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-026", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "enough time for a controller to drop the faulty feed", "title": "Engineering says the pump failed", "body": "Our Engineering page gives a cause this page never mentions and then refuses your reading of it: \"the recirculation pump had failed, and swapping it knocked the optical axis\", and \"We would rather put it that way than the way we first wrote it, which was that the photometer detected the pump dying: nobody was rostered to look at the trace daily, and the fault was noticed late.\" Your sentence says the warning was enough time to act. Their sentence says nobody acted. Write the version both pages can stand behind.", "after": "enough time for a controller to drop the faulty feed, although in this run nobody was watching the trace and the fault was found late (see Engineering)", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-027", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A standard step detector (any jump above 0.05 OD) flagged 23% of sensor anomalies and 0.8% of biological shifts", "title": "You scored the obvious alternative", "body": "Not one of the eight winning software pages compares its tool against a simpler method. You run the naive step detector on the same data and report that it cannot separate the two kinds of event. That one sentence is worth more than the 26.7 hours, because it says what the trust layer adds.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL proves its material works by comparing it against water, with the test and the p values. A baseline is the cheapest validation there is, and you have one.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-028", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Contamination found no support; the culture still smelled of B. subtilis and four-quadrant streaks gave 168 morphology", "title": "Say what each check ruled out", "body": "Two checks are named and neither says what it excludes. A smell test and a streak plate rule out different things, and a judge grading controls needs the clause. Smell is also not a method a reader can repeat without knowing what you were comparing it to.", "after": "Contamination found no support. Four-quadrant streaks gave 168 colony morphology with no second morphology, which rules out a gross bacterial contaminant; the culture also still had the characteristic B. subtilis odour, which is weaker evidence and is reported as such.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2023-ug-wm-04"], "excerpt_note": "William-and-Mary gives each control a rule attached to it: a negative control with plaques voids the whole session. One clause per control is enough.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-029", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "On the 434-hour run this gave 26.7 hours to mark OD unreliable and pause forecasts while the readings still looked normal", "title": "Put the n where the number is", "body": "The honest bound is in the next sentence, which is the right thing to have written. Move the scope into the sentence that makes the claim, so a judge skimming the section cannot take 26.7 hours as a capability.", "after": "On this one run, with one instrument event, the trust layer gave 26.7 hours to mark OD unreliable and pause forecasts while the readings still looked normal.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL never states a result without its n in the same sentence. It costs four words and it closes the question a judge would otherwise ask.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-030", "type": "praise", "anchor": "It has been shown on one run with one event of each kind; a detection rate needs deliberate faults on unseen batches", "title": "You name the experiment you have not run", "body": "This bounds the claim precisely and says what would settle it. Teams usually either overclaim or go quiet; you do neither. A judge reads this as a team that knows what its own evidence is worth.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL says its own engineered protein cannot be confirmed statistically, then names the likely cause. Same discipline, and both teams won on it."}, {"id": "dt-031", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "was logged for 3.2 h to OD 0.24, then went unrecorded for 10.2 h, so most of the exponential phase is in the gap", "title": "Two holes in one dataset, named apart", "body": "You say the exponential phase is missing here, and your Current limits say that most of this run sits above the photometer's linear ceiling, so R4 forecasts the photometer reading and not biomass. Both facts belong where the result is claimed. A judge who meets them only in the limits list will feel they were held back.", "after": "was logged for 3.2 h to OD 0.24, then went unrecorded for 10.2 h, so most of the exponential phase is in the gap. Most of the logged range also sits above the photometer's OD 1.2 linear ceiling on this path, so the forecasts below are forecasts of the photometer reading and not of biomass.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-032", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A forecast was made every hour from 12 h, each using only data up to that point.", "title": "Walk-forward stated in one line", "body": "This sentence is what stops a reader asking whether the filter saw the future. Thirteen words, and it makes the whole table believable. Judges look for exactly this clause and usually do not find it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-033", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Global linear and exponential fits did worse than persistence at short horizons.", "title": "The fold below says this better", "body": "The fold directly under this sentence explains why global linear and exponential fall behind, in more detail and more clearly. Keeping both means the reader meets the same fact twice in twenty words.", "after": "A two-state Kalman filter (level and slope) beats persistence at every horizon, by less as the horizon grows.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-034", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "grew in light-programmable array wells under six light arms, read on a plate reader at five time points to 24 h", "title": "Our plate map has one red intensity", "body": "Your six arms are weak, medium and high red and the same three greens. Our Measurement page's plate map for this run says: \"red at 168 lux in columns 1, 3 and 5, and green at 220, 510 and 3850 lux in columns 2, 4 and 6\". If all three red columns sat at 168 lux, then R1, R2 and R3 are replicates of one condition and not three light levels, which changes what leaving one out tests.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-035", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "A logistic model beat logistic-with-lag by AICc and fits at NRMSE 1.9%", "title": "AICc and NRMSE, both unexplained", "body": "Two model-selection terms in one clause, neither expanded, in the first sentence of the section. A judge from outside modelling stops reading here. Six words of gloss keeps them.", "after": "A logistic model fitted the data better than a logistic model with a lag phase, by AICc, which penalises the extra parameter. It fits at 1.9% normalised root-mean-square error (R² 0.997), against the 15% criterion for bioprocess models [3]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-036", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Green induction costs no growth: r spans 0.279 to 0.317 h⁻¹ across the six arms, within one standard error", "title": "A strong claim, bounded two folds later", "body": "This reads as a general finding about green light, and the fold below says the six arms are near-identical so the test cannot speak to a new condition. The bound belongs in the same breath as the claim.", "after": "Across these six light arms, green induction costs no growth: r spans 0.279 to 0.317 h⁻¹, within one standard error, and the highest-green arm reaches the highest plateau. The arms are biologically near-identical, so this is a reproducibility result and not a test of a new condition.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-037", "type": "praise", "anchor": "we left out each arm in turn, fitted the other five, and predicted its 24 h OD from its first 8 hours", "title": "Leave-one-out, with the table to prove it", "body": "Not one of the fourteen winning pages we read holds data back from its own fit. You do, you give all six arms, and you give the prediction with and without the prior side by side so the reader can see what the prior buys. This is the strongest single piece of evidence on the page.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-04"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg's structure benchmark is the closest any winner comes, and it has no held-out set and no comparison condition. Yours has both.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-038", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "With the prior the error was at most 7%; without it, 29 to 68%, from the same eight hours.", "title": "Measurement rejects this whole row", "body": "Your table gives the weak and medium green arms 0.958 and 0.975 at 24 h. Our Measurement page's table for the same 19 September run gives green at 220 and 510 lux as \"0.056, 0.083, 0.081\" and \"0.059, 0.008, 0.036\", and says: \"Cultures do not go backwards, so that row is a reading or a handling fault, not a result, and the run has not been repeated.\" R5 is the strongest evidence on the page and it rests on the row Measurement throws away. Resolve this before the freeze.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-039", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "A reactor run takes days; a plate run takes 24 hours and tests six conditions.", "title": "The reason for this test arrives last", "body": "This is why the plate prior exists, and it sits in the closing paragraph after the whole experiment. Move it to the top of the section so the reader knows what the test is for before reading the table.", "after": "A reactor run takes days; a plate run takes 24 hours and tests six conditions, so a prior fitted on a plate would let the twin forecast from the first few hours, when R4's Kalman filter has least to work with. On 19 September 2026, B. subtilis WB800 grew in light-programmable array wells...", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-01"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius puts the reason the tool exists in sentence two. The casebook's commonest fault among winners is that reason arriving late."}, {"id": "dt-040", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Both sensors were held in air beside each other, with no leaf.", "title": "Three names for the sensing surface", "body": "Here it is \"no leaf\", the channel table calls it a \"dummy-leaf sensor\", and the limits say \"it reads a dummy leaf\". A judge needs one sentence saying what the sensor actually senses, because the whole water-stress signal depends on it.", "after": "Both sensors were held in air beside each other. The SN-3001-YM-N01 measures the temperature of its own white plastic disc, which stands in for a leaf, so in this test it was reading air and not a plant.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-041", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Each site had three five-minute runs logged every five seconds, giving 540 paired readings.", "title": "Replicates and sampling rate, up front", "body": "Three sites, three runs each, a sampling interval and the total, in one sentence before any result. This is the only section of the page whose n is where a judge will look for it. It is the newest writing on the page and the best-reported.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02", "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL's n and KULeuven's listed concentrations do the same job. R6 is already at winner level on this question; the older sections are not.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-042", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Sharing the same air, the two sensors should agree, yet the leaf sensor read 1.2 to 1.9 °C warmer once settled", "title": "Expectation, result and consequence in one", "body": "You state what should have happened, what did happen, and what follows for the fix (one offset will not do), in a single sentence. This is a calibration that failed, reported in the section that uses the instrument instead of hidden in a limits list. All three of the strongest measurement winners report their failures exactly here.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03", "measurement-2023-ug-wm-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL's SRSF1 paragraph and William-and-Mary's dye slides are the same shape: the number, what it was measured against, and what it means for the decision.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-043", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the gap opened from −0.42 °C at the start of run 1 to +1.11 °C at the end of run 3", "title": "These are not the run means", "body": "The table two paragraphs above gives the room's three runs as −0.09, +0.54 and +0.97. These two numbers are the first and last readings inside the runs, which is a different thing, and nothing says so. A reader checking the table against this sentence finds four numbers that do not match.", "after": "the gap opened from −0.42 °C in the first readings of run 1 to +1.11 °C in the last readings of run 3, against run means of −0.09, +0.54 and +0.97", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-044", "type": "cut", "anchor": "That leaves two readings of the same data, and this test cannot separate them", "title": "The same alternative, told twice", "body": "Your Must-have table says this already: \"separates a site-dependent bias from an unfinished warm-up\". Two full sentences here and a table row there is one too many. Keep the short version here and let the table carry the fix.", "after": "Five minutes is not long enough for it to settle, so this test cannot tell a site-dependent offset from a sensor still warming from cold. Deciding between them needs a logged start time and a longer run.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-045", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Leaf wetness read 0.0% in every sample. The sensor was never wetted", "title": "A channel reported as untested", "body": "Reporting a flat zero and then saying the test cannot show whether the channel responds is harder to write than it looks. Most teams would have called 0.0% a dry reading and moved on. Keep both sentences; they are the ones a measurement judge will believe you on.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-og-aalto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aalto reports a whole measurement as below the detection limit and excludes it from the arithmetic. Yours is the same refusal to let a number stand for more than it shows.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-046", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Here, the SHT31 checks the leaf thermistor because both sensors sit in the same air.", "title": "The section's thesis is its last line", "body": "The idea that this test is R3's trust layer applied to a second instrument is the most interesting thing in R6, and it is at the bottom. A judge who stops after the table misses it. Open the section with it.", "after": "This test uses the same idea as the trust layer in R3: give an instrument a companion that cannot see what it measures. There, the reference beam checks the photometer because it never passes through the culture. Here, the SHT31 checks the leaf thermistor because both sensors sit in the same air.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2023-og-estonia-03"], "excerpt_note": "Estonia cuts the method loose from the project in one sentence. Yours does it too, and it belongs at the top of R6 instead of the bottom.", "ballot": "Could it be useful to other projects?"}, {"id": "dt-047", "type": "praise", "anchor": "To test the twin's forecast, we registered it before a reactor run of B. subtilis WB800 with constitutive ACC deaminase secretion", "title": "Nobody else in this prize does this", "body": "A forecast fixed by hash before the run, with its pass criteria in a table and the promise that the score goes in whichever way it falls. None of the eight Best Software winners and none of the six Best Measurement winners we read registered a prediction. Keep the hash, keep the table, and keep the promise.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-04"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg states its pass criteria before the score, which is the closest any winner comes. You state yours before the experiment.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-048", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The LPA run's temperature was not recorded", "title": "Our own protocol gives the plate a temperature", "body": "Our Measurement page's LPA procedure says \"Prewarm the media to 37 °C\" and \"Start the light program, incubate at 37 °C, shaking, for 8 to 10 hours total.\" This sentence is the reason the registered forecast excuses itself from a 37 °C reactor batch. If the plate also ran at 37 °C, that reason disappears and the criterion applies after all. Check the incubator setting and rewrite this clause either way.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-049", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The same batch carries the first ACC deaminase activity assay", "title": "An earlier activity assay already exists", "body": "Our Math Model page reports \"DNPH ACC deaminase activity assay, 2026-10-07, 7.2514 mU mL⁻¹ supernatant\", and Development describes the same measurement. That is the day before this batch, so this is not the first. Measurement also says the readout had to be Berthelot ammonium because DNPH was unavailable, while your criterion is written in α-ketobutyrate. Say which assay and which readout you mean.", "after": "The same batch carries the first ACC deaminase activity assay run on reactor permeate, with its bar set in advance", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-050", "type": "cut", "anchor": "ELISA follows on the CcaS/CcaR version once the western blot band is strong enough.", "title": "The assay plan belongs on Measurement", "body": "This fold is the only wet-lab assay on a software page, it has no result yet, and it ends in a plan for a second assay that depends on a third. Keep the one line that ties the assay to this batch and send the rest to Measurement, which the page already points to.", "after": "The same batch carries the first ACC deaminase activity assay, with its bar set in advance: at least 1 mU/mL in cell-free permeate, n = 3, against an empty-vector permeate and a minus-ACC blank. The assay and its follow-ups are on Measurement.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-051", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Firmware v3.10.9 · backend v4.12 · interface v4.13", "title": "Three versions, no components", "body": "You give three version numbers and never say what the three pieces are, what each one owns, or how they talk to each other. A judge cannot draw an architecture diagram from this page, and the software ballot asks for one. You already know the split, because the interlocks paragraph depends on it.", "after": "Firmware v3.10.9 on the Uno Q microcontroller reads the instruments and holds the interlocks. Backend v4.12 runs on the Uno Q's Linux side, serves the page on port 7000 and writes the log to disk. Interface v4.13 is one HTML file in the browser, which displays and commands and protects nothing.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-02"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius gives every component its name, its technology and a verb, so the diagram is readable in prose. Three sentences would do the same for you.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-052", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "One file, no install, served by the board on port 7000 in English or Traditional Chinese", "title": "No route for a judge to the code", "body": "This describes the farmer's experience, not a reader's. There is no clone URL, no dependency list, no command and nothing a judge can open on their own machine, and the Best Software rules make an open-source repository in iGEM's GitLab an eligibility item. Five of the eight winners print the clone URL and the command in the body. You have a versioned zip, a README and a licence; print them here.", "after": "One file, no install, served by the board on port 7000 in English or Traditional Chinese, with an explanation and normal range behind every information mark. The source is at [repository URL] under the MIT licence; clone it and copy the sketch to an Uno Q board, or open [file] in a browser to see the interface against recorded data with no board attached.", "excerpts": ["software-2023-og-ins-01", "software-2024-og-unm-01"], "excerpt_note": "INSAENSLyon1 writes its install section for a biologist who has never opened a terminal. UniMuenster says in the abstract which platforms it runs on and how it is distributed. Both are a paragraph each.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-053", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Derived against the provisional baseline: wall shear 4996 s⁻¹, ΔP ratio 0.83, γ ratio 1.01", "title": "Three terms the reader has not met", "body": "ΔP ratio and γ ratio appear here for the first time and are only defined two folds later, and \"the provisional baseline\" is explained in section 6. The paragraph that follows is about these two numbers disagreeing, so the reader needs them first.", "after": "Derived: wall shear 4996 s⁻¹, and two clogging indicators, the ΔP ratio at 0.83 and the γ ratio at 1.01. Both divide today's reading by a clean-module baseline that has not been re-measured on this plumbing, so both are marked provisional.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-054", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "After a restart the app appends to the same file and records the gap in a gap_s column.", "title": "The log format is never written out", "body": "Your provenance scheme is better than anything in the winners' pages and the reader never sees a column, a unit or a header line. The gap_s column is named once, in a subordinate clause. The app has a fixed CSV header; paste it, or list the columns with their units, and the software ballot's standards and integration questions are both answered in one table.", "after": "After a restart the app appends to the same file and records the gap in a gap_s column. The header is fixed across versions, so a September run can be pooled with an October one: timestamp_s, gap_s, od600_au, ph, p_in_kpa, p_out_kpa, p_shell_kpa, flow_ml_min, temp_c, temp_source, and the algorithm and constants that produced each row.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-og-unm-02", "software-2025-og-mun-01"], "excerpt_note": "UniMuenster makes interoperability a consequence of writing CSV between modules, in one sentence. Munich names its formats so a reader can check them. Neither claims compatibility as an adjective.", "ballot": "How well is the software compatible with, and does it leverage, existing synthetic biology standards? (e.g. SBOL, other RFCs, data formats, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-055", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "1007UI.html · MIT licence Open full screen", "title": "The repository licence is not MIT", "body": "This label says MIT. The repository's own LICENSE file says: \"This work — including the documentation, source code, and CAD files in this repository — is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\", and every page footer repeats it. This matters more than a mismatch: CC BY is not an OSI-approved open-source licence, and Best Software eligibility asks for one. Two of the eight winners made the same mistake, so getting it right puts you ahead of them. Pick MIT, add a LICENSE file to the code, and give the licence its own heading instead of a figure label.", "excerpts": ["software-2023-ug-fud-01"], "excerpt_note": "Fudan names the artefact behind every claim. A licence is an artefact: name it, and put the file where the name points.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-056", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The rig app's interlocks, board-side recording, bubble rejection and calibration bookkeeping each exist because a run failed without them", "title": "Your best argument is the last line", "body": "This sentence explains why the whole section exists, and it is at the bottom of the page's second-longest section. No winning page has a sentence like it. Open section 5 with it.", "after": "Every feature in the rig app is there because a run failed without it: the interlocks, the board-side recording, the bubble rejection and the calibration bookkeeping. Every number on this page was recorded by that one program, the Arduino App Lab application our instructor Chars wrote for the Uno Q board on BR-01.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-01"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg opens on its own measured failure, 27% of combinations active, and the tool arrives as the answer to it. Same structure, and yours covers four features at once."}, {"id": "dt-057", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "That makes it a digital shadow with actuation [1, 2].", "title": "The page is titled Digital Twin", "body": "Your own fold in section 1 defines a shadow as a system where changes back still need a person, and a twin as one where they do not. This bullet says the project is the first. A judge who has read both will want the page to say so near the top, not in the eighth limit. Being a shadow and saying so is a stronger position than a twin claim the page withdraws.", "after": "The loop is not closed. Valves move under software control, but every decision about the culture, above all induction, still goes through a person, so by the definition in section 1 this is a digital shadow with actuation and not yet a twin [1, 2].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-058", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "A documented two-line ASCII protocol with a heartbeat watchdog, interlocks on the microcontroller", "title": "The protocol is named, never shown", "body": "This is the only mention of the protocol and the harness on the page, and it is a bullet in the last section. A judge grading integration wants two lines of it: one command out, one reply in. You say it is documented, so the lines exist. Show them, and say what the harness covers and the command that runs it.", "after": "The serial protocol and test harness. Two ASCII lines carry everything: the board sends `D,1738.2,0.1758,6.500,...` once a window, and the page sends `C,pump,145` back, with a heartbeat that latches a fault if either side goes quiet for [number] ms. The desktop harness replays a recorded log against the page and the firmware with no board attached, and runs with [command].", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-02", "software-2025-og-mun-01"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius publishes its API spec as a file; Munich prints curl calls. The route in and the route out, written as something you could type, is what this ballot question rewards.", "ballot": "How well can the software be integrated with external tools/software applications? (APIs, packages, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-059", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The pressure logging also gives the project's cheapest biocontainment layer.", "title": "This fold belongs on Safety", "body": "The page already points at Safety for the other three containment layers, and this fold argues a safety case in a section about what other teams can reuse. The reusable part is one clause: pressure transducers you already log will show a broken fibre.", "after": "(keep one line in the For other teams list: \"Pressure logging as containment monitoring. A broken fibre shows up as a trans-membrane pressure anomaly, and those transducers are already logged, so detection is a software change. The other three layers are on Safety.\")", "excerpts": []}], "excerpts": {"measurement-2023-og-estonia-03": {"id": "measurement-2023-og-estonia-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Estonia-TUIT", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/estonia-tuit/measurement", "section": "ABSTRACT", "function": "reuse", "text": "This approach promises immediate, quantifiable siRNA assessment, cost-efficiency, scalability, and circumvents ethical concerns associated with animal testing. While we use this approach to address the global bee population decline caused by DWV, this system could be modified to optimize shRNAs or siRNAs for any desirable target.", "why_it_works": "One sentence separates the method from the project it was built for, which is what lets another team see themselves using it.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2023-ug-wm-01": {"id": "measurement-2023-ug-wm-01", "prize": "measurement", "team": "William-and-Mary", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/measurement", "section": "Summary", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Measurement in soil synthetic biology experiments, compared to laboratory experiments in a flask or tube, faces many challenges in accuracy and quantitation. First, bacteria tend to attach to soil particles, and the opacity and autofluorescence of these soil particles interferes with fluorescence-based methods, such as spectrometry and fluorescence microscopy. Separation procedures are often unable to capture the whole bacterial community, and are biased towards specific taxa and metabolic states. Thus, our project focused on conducting multiple measurement approaches for each variable that was examined.", "why_it_works": "The opening is about why measurement is hard here specifically, and the strategy falls out of the difficulty rather than being announced.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2023-ug-wm-02": {"id": "measurement-2023-ug-wm-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "William-and-Mary", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/measurement", "section": "Direct Microscopic Enumeration and Fluorescent Intensity Quantitation", "function": "limitation", "text": "We set the lower threshold higher than typical TRITC fluorescence intensity values of M. smegmatis pMLCherry (integrated) and M. smegmatis untransformed, but lower than typical TRITC fluorescence intensity values for M. smegmatis pCherry3. This statistical insignificance is representative of the limitation of the analysis method, not of biological results,since we know that M. smegmatis untransformed in sterile soil and M. smegmatis pCherry3 in sterile soil microcosms contained some level of bacteria, confirmed by plating.", "why_it_works": "A non-significant result is attributed to the analysis method and not to biology, with the independent measurement that proves which it was.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2023-ug-wm-04": {"id": "measurement-2023-ug-wm-04", "prize": "measurement", "team": "William-and-Mary", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/measurement", "section": "Direct Plating of Bacteria and Phage", "function": "method", "text": "First, for all plaque assays, we included a negative control of solely untransformed M. smegmatis; any results indicating plaques on a negative control caused all results from that plating session to not be included in our data analysis. All plates were also reviewed by at least two different independent sources, one being Dr. Saha whom has extensive experience in reviewing counts on phage plates. If there were any disagreements between the independent reviewers they discussed to resolve them, and if they couldn’t reach an agreement we averaged their estimated values.", "why_it_works": "Three rules that make hand counting defensible: a negative control that can void a session, two independent readers, and a stated way to settle disagreements.", "teaches": ["evidence", "consistency", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03": {"id": "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "KULeuven", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/kuleuven/measurement", "section": "3.3.1 Standard Caliberation Curve", "function": "validation", "text": "To quantify the concentration of metal ions in bacterial samples, a standard calibration curve was generated using known concentrations of Cu ions (25, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 250 and 300 mg/L) . The standard solutions were diluted from a stock solution in 0.85% saline to ensure consistency with the sample matrix, as bacterial samples would also be suspended in saline.", "why_it_works": "The standards are made up in the same matrix as the samples, and the sentence says why, which is the difference between a curve that transfers and one that does not.", "teaches": ["evidence", "consistency"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02": {"id": "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Austin-utexas", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/austin-utexas/measurement", "section": "OD660 to Cell Count Standard Calibration Curve", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "Additionally, Figure 2B presents the same data plotted on a logarithmic scale, which helps to visualize lower particle concentrations. It highlights how the sensitivity of OD600 measurements decreases at lower particle counts around an OD600 value of 0.1. This indicates that our plate reader may not provide accurate readings for values at or below OD600 of 0.1.", "why_it_works": "They state where their own instrument stops being trustworthy and give the number, which turns a calibration curve into a usable range.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04": {"id": "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Austin-utexas", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/austin-utexas/measurement", "section": "OD660 to Cell Count Standard Calibration Curve", "function": "assumption", "text": "Although particle count does value is not directly equivalent to cell count, an iGEM Interlab study found that “based on our results, we recommend calibrating OD to estimated cell count using serial dilution of silica microspheres, which produces highly precise calibration”5. Thus, we used the particle count per OD600 value as our cell count per OD600.", "why_it_works": "The substitution of particle count for cell count is flagged as a substitution and sourced, rather than being quietly folded into the conversion.", "teaches": ["evidence", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02": {"id": "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/measurement", "section": "In-vitro Antibody Interference Measurement: Indirect ELISA", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "For example, our leftmost standard (0.1 ng/mL) gives us an idea of the lowest IF concentration that can be reliably detected above background. So, values that are much lower than this are not confidently distinguishable from background absorbance. Conversely, the highest standard (50 ng/mL) approaches the upper plateau of the curve, where the assay starts to saturate. As can be seen from the graph, the absorbance here is maximal; meaning higher concentrations will not give proportionally higher signals and should be diluted before being quantified.", "why_it_works": "The calibration curve is read out loud at both ends: the lowest point that beats background and the concentration where the assay saturates, with the instruction that follows from each.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-og-aalto-03": {"id": "measurement-2025-og-aalto-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/measurement", "section": "Elemental Analysis: Microwave Plasma Atomic Emission Spectroscopy (MP-AES) for Cobalt Quantification in Bound B12", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "MP‑AES analysis of nitric acid digests prepared from our intrinsic factor–vitamin B12 binding reactions produced cobalt signals below the reliable detection capability of method on the instrument used, with readbacks typically in the 20–30 µg/L range but a validated practical LOD of approximately 50 µg/L in this proteinaceous, acidified matrix; therefore, cobalt concentrations for all samples are reported as \"< 50 µg/L (LOQ)\" with no numeric values used in calculations or comparisons", "why_it_works": "A whole measurement came back under the detection limit and is reported as a censored value, with the readbacks shown and explicitly excluded from any arithmetic.", "teaches": ["evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-og-aalto-04": {"id": "measurement-2025-og-aalto-04", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/measurement", "section": "IF-Vitamin B12 Binding Assay", "function": "reuse", "text": "Low protein concentrations in the recombinant IF preparations initially led to inconsistent spectrophotometer readings, increasing the sample measurement volume from 1.5 µL to 2 µL improved reproducibility. Buffer composition was another critical factor, in which the initial HEPES-based binding buffer yielded poor blank readings, and switching to alternative formulations such as Tris + NaCl did not improve results. Ultimately, re-adjusting the pH of the original HEPES buffer restored clean baselines, highlighting the importance of precise pH control.", "why_it_works": "Each failure is logged as symptom, change, result, which is what the next team needs and what a clean protocol alone would hide.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02": {"id": "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/epfl/measurement", "section": "Quantitative Analysis", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To assess the contribution of individual components, partial formulations (cellulose alone; cellulose + silica) and a protein lysate control were compared against water (n = 3 each). None of these conditions differed significantly from water following Bonferroni correction (p = 0.1023 for cellulose; p = 0.4705 for cellulose + silica; p = 1.0000 for lysate).", "why_it_works": "A flat null result carries its n, its correction for multiple comparisons and each exact p value, so the reader can check the claim rather than take it.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03": {"id": "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/epfl/measurement", "section": "Quantitative Analysis", "function": "limitation", "text": "Thus, under the current experimental design and limited sample size, the protective contribution of SRSF1 cannot be confirmed statistically. Notably, the lysate condition displayed a relatively large measurement error, which may reflect uncontrolled sources of variability such as fluctuations in wind or flame intensity.", "why_it_works": "The team's own engineered protein failed to beat the control and they say so in one sentence, then name the likely source of the noise instead of explaining the result away.", "teaches": ["evidence", "voice", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "software-2023-og-ins-01": {"id": "software-2023-og-ins-01", "prize": "software", "team": "INSAENSLyon1", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/insaenslyon1/software", "section": "How to install it", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "A command prompt, often referred to as 'cmd,' is a means of interacting with your computer, a daily task performed with a slightly less user-friendly interface compared to the graphical interface. However, don't worry, we will provide you with all the instructions you need to use it in order to access our app!", "why_it_works": "Assumes the reader has never opened a terminal, says so without condescension, and teaches the four commands they will need. The install section is written for the biologist who will actually run the tool.", "teaches": ["audience", "clarity", "documentation"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2023-ug-fud-01": {"id": "software-2023-ug-fud-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Fudan", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/fudan/software", "section": "Overview", "function": "reuse", "text": "To make RAP user-friendly, we have designed an intuitive Web UI with tutorials; and for advanced usage of RAP, we have provided a series of RESTFul APIs. To compatible with existing sequence manipulation tools, we have adopted the GenBank format (opens new window) to save sequences, which is an existing standards, and the sequences can be used in external tools such as SnapGene (opens new window). In order to get feedback from external users to improve user-friendliness, we have collaborated with other iGEM teams (SCUT-China iGEM 2023 (opens new window) and Nanjing-China iGEM 2023 (opens new window)) to improve the software. Meanwhile, sequences predicted and generated by our RAP, has been validated by experiments (opens new window).", "why_it_works": "Four ballot questions answered in one paragraph, each with a named artefact: a Web UI and tutorials, RESTful APIs, the GenBank format and SnapGene, two named iGEM teams who used the tool and fed back, and wet-lab validation of its output. Nothing here is a claim without a referent.", "teaches": ["reuse", "evidence", "standards", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-og-unm-01": {"id": "software-2024-og-unm-01", "prize": "software", "team": "UniMuenster", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/unimuenster/software", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Our tool allows access to machine learning assisted protein engineering in low-throughput settings. It can help you in case you too want to improve some protein’s activity, stability or any other characteristic, but are limited in your measuring capability to only few rounds of a small number of measurements. The command line tool works on Windows, Linux and MacOS and is distributed as an easy-to-install python package.", "why_it_works": "Written in the second person to the reader who might install it: the situation you are in, what the tool does about it, the platforms it runs on and how it is distributed. A judge who will not install anything still learns what it is and who it is for.", "teaches": ["audience", "clarity", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-og-unm-02": {"id": "software-2024-og-unm-02", "prize": "software", "team": "UniMuenster", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/unimuenster/software", "section": "Introduction", "function": "validation", "text": "We took a modular implementation approach for easy expandability and used the Typer library to bundle our software into a tool which can comfortably be used through the command line. Our modules communicate through easy-to-parse .csv files, so at any step it is possible to use some different tool and inject the results into our workflow. To ensure the quality of our tutorial we tested it both for Windows and Linux, and additionally tasked one of our team members, who didn’t have any python or command line experience, with following the installation guide and all the usage examples, to great success.", "why_it_works": "User-friendliness is tested rather than claimed: the install guide was run on two operating systems and then handed to a team member with no Python or command-line experience. The evidence is a person, named by their lack of expertise.", "teaches": ["validation", "audience", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-ug-vln-01": {"id": "software-2024-ug-vln-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Vilnius-Lithuania", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software", "section": "Introduction", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Monitoring and ensuring optimal bacterial growth conditions can often be a time-consuming task due to the long periods required for bacteria to grow and replicate. This was no exception in our wet lab activities, as our bioreactor required constant supervision and in-person presence in the lab. Additionally, identifying problems and when they occurred was challenging. To address this, we developed a remote interface for the bioreactor, reducing the time required for supervision and automating data collection by storing bioreactor readings in a remote database.", "why_it_works": "Opens on the lab problem, not the software: bacteria take a long time to grow, so someone had to sit with the bioreactor, and faults were hard to place in time. The tool is introduced as the answer to that in one sentence.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "audience"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-ug-vln-02": {"id": "software-2024-ug-vln-02", "prize": "software", "team": "Vilnius-Lithuania", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software", "section": "Architecture", "function": "method", "text": "- PostgreSQL Database: This relational database stores all bioreactor measurements and control settings, ensuring data persistence and easy access for both users and automated processes.\n- Python Flask Server: Acts as the backend, providing RESTful endpoints to read and write data to the database. Additionally, it handles automated processes such as sending alert emails when bioreactor readings exceed preset thresholds.\n- Arduino Client: The microcontroller in the bioreactor communicates with the system through REST APIs, sending real-time measurements (temperature, pH, turbidity) to the server and fetching updated control values like mixing speed and temperature settings. This integration allows the hardware to operate in sync with user inputs.", "why_it_works": "Each component gets its name, its technology and the job it does, and the microcontroller entry says exactly how the hardware and the server talk. An architecture diagram is readable in prose because every box has a verb.", "teaches": ["architecture", "clarity", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-ug-vln-03": {"id": "software-2024-ug-vln-03", "prize": "software", "team": "Vilnius-Lithuania", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software", "section": "Conclusion", "function": "limitation", "text": "Out of the five planned MVPs, we successfully implemented four. Unfortunately, MVP5, which involved the glucose monitoring system, was incomplete due to time constraints and complexity. However, the current system provides a robust and efficient solution for remotely monitoring and controlling the bioreactor.\nSoftware like this is oftentimes pricey, which many projects can not afford. Our software is designed to be compatible with any bioreactor, making it easily accessible for future iGEM teams. This adaptability ensures that future teams can integrate the software into their projects without extensive customization.", "why_it_works": "Counts the planned features, says four of five shipped, and names the one that did not and why. The reuse claim in the second half ('compatible with any bioreactor') is asserted with no test behind it, which is the weaker half of the same paragraph.", "teaches": ["limitation", "honesty", "overclaiming"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2025-og-mrb-01": {"id": "software-2025-og-mrb-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/marburg/software", "section": "mATChmaker", "function": "page-opening", "text": "We observed the same limitation in our project — while we successfully generated numerous hybrid NRPSs, many failed to produce detectable peptides. Specifically, during our first attempt at derivatization by Golden Gate cloning, only 27 % of combinations were catalytically active. Knowing beforehand which units work together and which do not would save us and other researchers a lot of time and resources and this is why we developed mATChmaker - a software tool to guide NRPS hybrid design.", "why_it_works": "Names the problem, then makes the team's own measured failure the reason the tool exists: 27 % of their hybrid combinations worked. A judge learns what the tool is for and why it was needed in three sentences, with a number doing the arguing.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2025-og-mrb-04": {"id": "software-2025-og-mrb-04", "prize": "software", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/marburg/software", "section": "Automated Structure Prediction", "function": "validation", "text": "Structures containing one of the remaining 24 donor units were evaluated using two criteria: The two T domains should be bound to opposite ends of the C domain and the Ppant arm substrates should extend into the C domain. 49 structures were correctly folded, representing 68 % of the 72 condensation complexes excluding split genes and epimerisation domains.", "why_it_works": "Gives the two pass criteria before the score, then the score with its denominator and the cases excluded from it. The 68 % is checkable because the reader knows what was counted and what was thrown out.", "teaches": ["evidence", "validation", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2025-og-mun-01": {"id": "software-2025-og-mun-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Munich", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/munich/software", "section": "Input-Output Pipeline", "function": "reuse", "text": "The tools output is composed of different files with customizable options. Industry-standard GenBank files compatible with all major molecular biology software including Geneious Prime, SnapGene and Benchling are the default. Each functional domain is properly annotated with feature tags for easy visualization and modification.\nAdditionally we provide the option of creating codon-optimized sequences for the target organism with avoided restriction sites for standard cloning methods these include: RFC10, RFC12, RFC1000, BioBrick standards, any custom restriction sites or a pre-assembled iGEM Registry Compatible Collection.", "why_it_works": "Answers the standards question by naming the formats and the standards by number (GenBank, RFC10, RFC12, RFC1000, BioBrick) and the three commercial tools the output opens in. Compatibility is shown as a list of names a reader can check, not as the word 'compatible'.", "teaches": ["standards", "reuse", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}}};
